-- title:
--   Two-component exhaustion at an ℓ-adic place of X₀(Nℓ)
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the `AlgebraicClosure` of $\mathbb{Q}$), let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $\ell$ a prime with $A$-valuation of $\ell$ less than $1$ (so $A$ has residue characteristic $\ell$) and with $\ell \nmid N$. Let $\sigma$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of the Laurent-series field `modularFunctionFieldFull (N * ℓ)` which is Atkin–Lehner in the sense of `IsAtkinLehnerAutFull N ℓ`: for every nonzero divisor $d$ of $N$ it interchanges the $q$-expansions $j(q^{d})$ and $j(q^{d\ell})$. Let $W$ be a place of the base-changed field $L =$ `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (modularFunctionFieldFull (N * ℓ))` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. a proper valuation subring of $L$ containing the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a principal ideal ring, with $W.\mathrm{ord}$ the associated normalised integer valuation. Assume $W$ is centred at elements of $A$ for both reciprocal $j$-functions: there are $c_1, c_2 \in A$ with $W.\mathrm{ord}(j^{-1} - c_1) > 0$ and $W.\mathrm{ord}\big((\bar\beta(j))^{-1} - c_2\big) > 0$, where $j$ is the element of $L$ given by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` and $\bar\beta =$ `heckeBetaBar` is the degeneracy map $x(q) \mapsto x(q^{\ell})$ from level $N$ to level $N\ell$. Let $e, f \in L$ be integral (annihilated by a monic polynomial) over the subring of $L$ generated by the image of $A$ together with $j^{-1}$ and $(\bar\beta(j))^{-1}$, and let $a, b \in A$ satisfy $W.\mathrm{ord}(e - a) > 0$ and $W.\mathrm{ord}(f - b) > 0$. Assume finally that every Laurent coefficient of $e$ has $A$-valuation less than $1$, and that every Laurent coefficient of the image of $f$ under the automorphism of $L$ induced by $\sigma$ via `geomAut` has $A$-valuation less than $1$. Then the $A$-valuation of $ab$ is less than $1$.
--
--   This is the Fourier-expansion form of the statement that the special fibre of $X_0(N\ell)$ at $\ell \nmid N$ is exhausted by its two components, as in Deligne–Rapoport: a place of the geometric function field centred where both $1/j$ and $1/j(q^{\ell})$ take values in $A$ lies on one of the two components, so residues coming from the two components cannot both be units. It is used in the identification of the cusp charts of the fibre model, in [`ModularCurve.CharPModel.FibreModel.spPlace_d1_of_cuspChart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.CharPModel.FibreModel.spPlace_d1_of_cuspChart) and its level-indexed variant.
